Why this project?
Remote systems are vital for safely managing explosives, toxins, and radiation, yet commercial robots remain prohibitively expensive and complex. This project demonstrates that a reliable, 5-DOF robotic arm can be built affordably using the ESP32 microcontroller. By utilizing a browser-based Wi-Fi interface, we eliminate the need for proprietary apps or controllers, creating a foundational prototype for ECE research and real-time IoT control.​

What makes our system different?
The system works as a standalone web server using the ESP32’s built-in Wi-Fi, so it doesn’t need internet or cloud services. It allows real-time control of servos through web sliders, with fast response (under 150 ms) using simple HTTP requests. It is built using easily available off-the-shelf components, making it simple to assemble and repair. The design is also scalable, so more servos or sensors can be added without major changes..

Vision of this Project:The vision of the Wi-Fi Controlled Robotic Arm project extends far beyond a laboratory prototype. It represents the beginning of a design philosophy: accessible, affordable, and deployable embedded robotics for real-world safety challenges

Abstract 
This project focuses on the design and implementation of a Wi-Fi controlled robotic arm using the ESP32 microcontroller. The system enables safe and remote handling of objects in hazardous or restricted environments through a wireless interface accessible from any smartphone or web browser. The ESP32 operates as both a Wi-Fi access point and an embedded web server, hosting a control panel with sliders for each joint of the robotic arm, including the base, shoulder, elbow, wrist, and gripper. User inputs from the web interface are sent as HTTP GET requests and are converted in real time into PWM signals to control the servo motors, allowing precise movement from 0° to 180°. A separate 5V power supply is used for the servos to maintain system stability. The system achieves a low latency of around 80–120 ms within a 20–30 meter range. Built using cost-effective components, the project demonstrates a scalable and practical solution for applications such as industrial automation, laboratory assistance, and educational robotics.
